    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Definitions of cadres included in the OptimizeMNH guidance",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-definitions-of-cadres-included-in-the-optimizemn.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Definitiona\nDifferent names\nAdvanced \nlevel associate \nclinicianb\nA professional clinician with advanced competencies to diagnose \nand manage the most common medical, maternal, child health and \nsurgical conditions, including obstetric and gynaecological surgery \n(e.g. caesarian sections). Advanced level associate clinicians are \ngenerally trained for 4 to 5 years post-secondary education in \nestablished higher education institutions and/or 3 years post initial \nassociate clinician training. The clinicians are registered and their \npractice is regulated by their national or subnational regulat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A table defining the health worker cadres included in the OptimizeMNH guidance, from advanced level associate clinicians and associate clinicians to auxiliary nurses, auxiliary nurse midwives, lay health workers, midwives, nurses and non-specialist doctors. For each it describes training, competencies and regulation and lists alternative titles used in different countries. Footnotes note that only trained traditional birth attendants were considered and that the WHO definition of midwife was used.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Executive summary",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-1-executive-summary.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Human resource shortages in the health services are widely \nacknowledged as a threat to the attainment of the health-\nrelated Millennium Development Goals (MDGs). Attempts to \noptimize the potential of the existing health workforce are \ntherefore crucial. A more rational distribution of tasks and \nresponsibilities among cadres of health workers is seen \nas a promising strategy for improving access and cost-\neffectiveness within health systems. For example, access to \ncare may be improved by training and enabling ‘mid-level’ \nand ‘lay’ health workers to perform specific interventions \nthat migh",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens the executive summary, stating that health workforce shortages threaten the health-related MDGs and that redistributing tasks among cadres, including mid-level and lay workers, is a promising way to improve access and cost-effectiveness. It notes the need to define which maternal and newborn interventions different cadres can deliver safely and effectively.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Objectives",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-objectives.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "These recommendations have been developed as part of the \nWorld Health Organization’s mandate to provide normative \nguidance to its member states. The objective of this guidance \nis to issue evidence-based recommendations to facilitate \nuniversal access to key, effective maternal and newborn \ninterventions through the optimization of health worker roles. \nThese recommendations are intended for health policy-\nmakers, managers and other stakeholders at a regional, \nnational and international level. By providing this broad \nguidance internationally, the World Health Organization (WHO) \nassumes th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that the guidance aims to provide evidence-based recommendations for universal access to key maternal and newborn interventions by optimizing health worker roles, for policy-makers and managers who will adapt them to their national context.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guidance development methods",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-guidance-development-methods.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The procedures used in the development of this guidance are \noutlined in the WHO handbook for guideline development (1). \nThese are: (i) the identification of questions related to \npractice and health policy for which answers are needed; \n(ii) the retrieval of up-to-date research-based evidence; \n(iii) the assessment and synthesis of evidence; (iv) the \nformulation of recommendations with inputs from a wide \nrange of stakeholders; and (v) the formulation of plans for \nthe dissemination, implementation, impact evaluation and \nupdating of the guidance. \nThe scientific evidence for these recommen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how the guidance was developed following the WHO handbook, using GRADE evidence profiles from systematic reviews complemented by qualitative and programme evidence. It explains the four categories of recommendation, from recommending the option to recommending against it, including options suggested only in rigorous research.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Recommendations",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "and approved by an international group of experts (the \nGuidance Panel) who participated in the WHO Technical \nConsultation on Optimizing the Delivery of Key Interventions \nto Attain MDGs 4 and 5 (OptimizeMNH) meeting held in \nGeneva, Switzerland from 16–19 April 2012 and on 26 June \n2012. The experts also identified important knowledge gaps \nthat need to be addressed through further primary research.\nRecommendations\nThe Guidance Panel made 119 recommendations: 36 for \nlay health workers (LHWs), 23 for auxiliary nurses (ANs), \n17 for auxiliary nurse midwives (ANMs), 13 for nurses, \n13 for midw",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports that an international Guidance Panel approved the recommendations at meetings in Geneva in April and June 2012, making 119 recommendations across cadres, from 36 for lay health workers to 1 for non-specialist doctors. It notes one excluded question on misoprostol self-administration and the removal of antiretroviral recommendations to align with other WHO guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Dissemination, adaptation and implementation of the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-dissemination-adaptation-and-implementation-of-t.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "implementation of the recommendations\nThe Guidance Panel acknowledged that health system \narrangements and specific sociocultural and political factors \nwill shape the implementation of the recommendations in \nspecific contexts. National dialogue is therefore needed, \nincluding discussions regarding whether the (non)availability \nof skilled health workers is a significant contributor to the \naccessibility and utilization of key interventions; whether \nthere is a willingness to consider ‘task shifting’ as a way \nto address existing problems; which of the health workers \nreferred to in this guid",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Stresses that implementation will depend on health system and sociocultural contexts and calls for national dialogue on whether shortages of skilled workers limit access and which cadres and intervention packages are suitable for task shifting. It advises treating individual recommendations as packages by condition and cadre.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Overview of judgements",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-overview-of-judgements.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The practices recommended by the Guidance Panel are listed \nbelow. The full list (which includes practices recommended \nonly in the context of rigorous research as well as practices \nnot recommended by the Guidance Panel) is presented in \nChapter 4 (‘Evidence and recommendations’). \nWe recommend the use of LAY HEALTH WORKERS to \ndeliver the following interventions:\n• The following promotional interventions for maternal and \nnewborn health: \n»»\nPromotion of appropriate care-seeking behaviour and \nantenatal care during pregnancy\n»»\nPromotion of companionship during labour\n»»\nPromotion of sleepin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the practices recommended by the Guidance Panel for each cadre, beginning with promotional and some clinical interventions for lay health workers, such as promoting antenatal care, birth preparedness, nutrition and family planning. It continues through auxiliary nurses, nurses, midwives and associate clinicians, covering tasks such as administering uterotonics, magnesium sulphate and caesarean sections by advanced level associate clinicians. It ends with external cephalic version by non-specialist doctors with targeted monitoring and evaluation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-2-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Many WHO Member States have attempted to increase \naccess to effective interventions. One way of facilitating \nimprovement is to give available cadres short periods \nof additional training and then to allow them to take on \nparticular activities they have not undertaken before. This \nprocess is known as ‘task shifting’ or ‘task sharing’ and \nis one of several strategies that can potentially improve \nthe utilization of health system resources. Ultimately, task \nshifting can also improve health system performance and \noutcomes. The terms that are used to describe these change \nprocesses often la",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces task shifting, or task sharing, as training cadres to take on tasks they did not previously perform in order to improve access and health system performance, noting that terms are used loosely. It places the guidance within WHO's GREAT project for developing evidence-based guidelines and supporting their adaptation and implementation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The global health workforce crisis",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-the-global-health-workforce-crisis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Widespread crises in the health workforce are impacting on \nthe realization of the health-related MDGs. According to \na 2006 World Health Report, 57 countries face chronic human \nresource shortages in the health sector. Typically, such \ncountries are LMICs and are nations with the highest burden \nof health problems such as HIV/AIDS and maternal and \nnewborn mortality (3).\nSuch problems are compounded by global and national \nimbalances in the distribution of the health workforce. \nNotably, 36 of the 57 countries currently facing health-related \nhuman resource crises are in sub-Saharan Africa. T",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the global health workforce crisis, noting that 57 countries face severe shortages, 36 of them in sub-Saharan Africa, which bears 24% of the global disease burden with 3% of the health workforce. It also highlights rural–urban imbalances and shortages of specialist doctors.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Human resource gaps in maternal and newborn health",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-human-resource-gaps-in-maternal-and-newborn-heal.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "newborn health\nThe low proportion of women assisted by skilled birth \nattendants is an important indicator of the global personnel \nshortage in the health sector. Approximately 60 million births \neach year occur in settings other than health facilities and \n52 million of these births take place without the support of \na skilled birth attendant (5). While skilled birth attendance is \nprovided at almost all births in most industrialized countries, \nfewer than 50% of births in the majority of countries in \nSouth Asia and sub-Saharan Africa receive such support (6). \nIn 2008, the WHO estimated tha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Uses skilled birth attendance to illustrate staffing gaps in maternal and newborn health, noting that about 52 million births a year occur without a skilled attendant and coverage is below 50% in much of South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa. It contrasts this with the high skilled attendance and physician density of countries with the lowest maternal and neonatal mortality.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Optimizing the roles of health workers through task shifting: Overarching principles",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-optimizing-the-roles-of-health-workers-through-t.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "through task shifting: Overarching \nprinciples\nNational health strategies help to facilitate both improved \nlevels of access and good quality care. Following the global \nendorsement of the MDGs, further efforts have been made in \nlow- and middle-income countries to improve maternal and \nnewborn health by improving levels of skilled birth attendance \nand institutional birth coverage. Attempts have also been \nmade to establish universal access to sexual and reproductive \nhealth and rights. \nThe recommendation that skilled birth attendants should \nsupport all births has been universally acknowled",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the overarching principles of WHO guidance on optimizing health worker roles through task shifting to improve access to maternal and newborn health interventions. It notes progress on skilled birth attendance since the MDGs and describes how some countries have trained lay health workers, defined as workers with intervention-specific training but no formal professional qualification. It places such programmes within the WHO health systems building blocks, shown in Figure 1.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Methods",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-3-methods.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guidance represents the WHO’s efforts to support the use \nof evidence-informed policies and practices in all countries. \nThe guidance forms part of the WHO’s Knowledge-to-Action \nGREAT project (10).\nThis guidance was developed using standardized operating \nprocedures in accordance with the processes described in the \nWHO handbook for guideline development (1) and draws on \nthe methods described in the Handbook for developing health \nsystems guidance (11). In summary, the process included: \n(i)\t Identifying critical questions and critical outcomes\n(ii)\t Retrieving evidence\n(iii)\tAssessing,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises how the guidance was developed under WHO guideline procedures and the GREAT project, from identifying critical questions to grading evidence, formulating recommendations and planning dissemination.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Identification of critical questions and critical outcomes",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-identification-of-critical-questions-and-critica.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "critical outcomes\nThe following steps were performed in order to identify \ncritical questions and outcomes for the guidance:\nAssessment of the need for guidance\nThe need for guidance was assessed in two ways:\na)\t A WHO guidance prioritization survey: An online survey \nwas conducted to identify WHO guidance priorities for \nmaternal and perinatal health. A survey was sent to \nWHO regional and country offices as well as to all \nnational Ministries of Health. In addition, the survey \nwas disseminated through other relevant networks \nand electronic forums. 393 responses were received, \nincluding 56",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how the need for the guidance was assessed through an online prioritization survey with 393 responses, most from low- and middle-income countries, and through requests from Member States, for example about lay health workers giving misoprostol. It explains how critical questions and outcomes were defined for each health worker cadre. It clarifies that changes requiring training that effectively creates a different cadre were not treated as task shifting.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Retrieval of the evidence",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-retrieval-of-the-evidence.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Three main types of evidence were considered:\n• Evidence on the effectiveness of the delivery of \ninterventions by specific cadres based on randomized \ncontrolled trials (RCTs), non-randomized controlled trials \n(n-RCTs), controlled before-and-after studies (CBAs), \ninterrupted time series studies (ITS), and cohort studies.\n• Evidence based on qualitative studies of factors affecting \nthe implementation of programmes to optimize the tasks \nand roles of cadres. \n• Evidence based on country case studies of factors \naffecting the implementation of large-scale programmes \nfor scaling up human reso",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the three types of evidence used: effectiveness studies of delivery by specific cadres, qualitative studies of implementation factors, and country case studies of scaling up health workforces. Cochrane systematic reviews were the main effectiveness source, with outdated reviews updated by their authors. A cross-cutting analysis of implementation factors was structured by the WHO health systems building blocks.",
