      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Access to commodities",
      "pageStart": 86,
      "pageEnd": 86,
      "target": "../sections/sec-032-access-to-commodities.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Drug and supply shortages may negatively impact \nprogramme implementation and task shifting. Inadequate \ntransport infrastructure may also affect the ability of health \nproviders to reach clients, the ability of district officers to \nprovide supervision to health providers, and the referral of \npatients to health facilities.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that drug and supply shortages and poor transport can undermine task shifting, supervision and referrals.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-033",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Service delivery",
      "pageStart": 86,
      "pageEnd": 88,
      "target": "../sections/sec-033-service-delivery.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The impact of task shifting on the roles and identities of \nhealth-care providers: Task shifting may result in changes \nto the meaning of particular provider roles within a broader \norganizational context, to working relationships, and to \nrelations between different components of a health system. \nThe shifting of more technological tasks to midwives, for \ninstance, may disrupt the relationships between midwives \nand patients as well as the provision of continuous care – \nelements which are central to most notions of what midwifery \nis. However, the shifting of tasks to lay health workers may",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how task shifting affects health workers' roles and identities, for example by disrupting continuity of care for midwives or raising lay health workers' status. It discusses providers' views, including role ambiguity, job satisfaction when supported and fear when training is lacking, and workload changes. It also covers recipients' views, noting that closer social distance can improve care but threaten confidentiality for stigmatized conditions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-034",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Health workforce",
      "pageStart": 88,
      "pageEnd": 89,
      "target": "../sections/sec-034-health-workforce.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Pre-service and in-service training: Problems include \nthe inadequate provision of training, training that does \nnot address practice needs, unskilled trainers, inflexible \ntraining schedules, and unrealistic and unachievable training \nrequirements. The reviews suggested that training needs \nto evolve in relation to changes in the roles of cadres or the \ntasks that they are expected to undertake.\nSupervision and support: In addition to clinical supervision, \nother forms of support need to be provided, including \nemotional support and advice related to liability issues. \nChallenges related to s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes workforce issues in task shifting, including inadequate or poorly matched training and weak supervision, and suggests field-based supervision and peer support.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-035",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Contextualizing guidance",
      "pageStart": 89,
      "pageEnd": 91,
      "target": "../sections/sec-035-6-contextualizing-guidance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Using evidence-informed global guidance, such as \nrecommendations issued by the WHO, can help countries \nto improve their health system performance. However, \ndeveloping or changing health policies and health systems \ninvolves complex processes. Most guidance documents do \nnot contextualize recommendations in relation to specific \nnational needs, particularly with regard to health and political \nsystems. This section therefore offers a brief overview of \nhow the OptimizeMNH guidance can be contextualized at a \nnational level using an 8-step ‘health systems guidance for \npolicy-making’ framewor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how countries can adapt the OptimizeMNH task-shifting guidance using an eight-step framework for health systems guidance in policy-making. Steps include identifying the national policy venue and timing, clarifying the problem and options, and considering health system and political contexts. It describes combining global evidence with local data and views in evidence briefs for policy dialogues.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-036",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Research implications",
      "pageStart": 91,
      "pageEnd": 93,
      "target": "../sections/sec-036-7-research-implications.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following general observations regarding future research \nwere made by the Guidance Panel during the meetings:\n• There was a general agreement that despite the \nattention given to task shifting and to lay health worker \nprogrammes in LMICs in particular, the paucity of reliable \nresearch data was alarming.\n• Most of the research evidence identified related to both \nbarriers and facilitators and the effectiveness of lay health \nworker programmes. Far less evidence about mid-level \nproviders was found, especially evidence related to \nauxiliary nurses, auxiliary nurse midwives and associate",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the Guidance Panel's observations on research needs, noting a worrying lack of reliable data on task shifting, especially for mid-level cadres such as auxiliary nurses and associate clinicians. It recommends qualitative research alongside effectiveness trials and evaluating packages of interventions and team models. It then poses specific research questions for each health worker cadre.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-037",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Dissemination of the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 93,
      "pageEnd": 95,
      "target": "../sections/sec-037-8-dissemination-of-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The ultimate goal of these recommendations is to improve the \nquality of care and health outcomes for mothers and infants. \nThe dissemination and implementation of this guidance are \ncrucial steps which should be undertaken by the international \ncommunity and national and local health care services. \nThe WHO Department of Reproductive Health and Research \nhas adopted a formal Knowledge-to-Action framework \nfor the dissemination, adaptation and implementation of \nthis guidance (10). In addition to this framework, a list of \npriority actions was agreed upon during the WHO technical \nconsultation",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes plans to disseminate the task-shifting recommendations through WHO's Knowledge-to-Action framework, WHO offices, ministries, UN agencies and NGOs, the WHO Reproductive Health Library and a policy brief. It stresses participatory national adaptation. It lists priority dissemination actions such as piloting a workbook and using online channels.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-77764/blocks/prose-sec-038",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Review and updating of the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 95,
      "pageEnd": 96,
      "target": "../sections/sec-038-9-review-and-updating-of-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The recommendations included in this guidance and the \nderivative products will be used in international and national \nadaptation initiatives. Feedback received will be recorded and \nused to contribute to future updates. Members of the WHO \nTechnical Secretariat and researchers from The Norwegian \nKnowledge Centre for the Health Services are currently \nconducting user testing of the evidence-to-recommendation \nframeworks outlined in this guidance. This testing will assess \nhow well these frameworks function as a process tool for \nthe presentation of evidence and decision-making within \nguidanc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that feedback from adapting the task-shifting recommendations will inform updates, that evidence-to-recommendation frameworks are being user-tested, and that the guidance will be updated by 2018 unless new evidence requires earlier change.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
